✅ Role of the OS module in Node.js
The OS (Operating System) module is a built-in Node.js module that helps us get information about the system (computer/server) on which our Node.js application is running.
In simple words:
👉 The OS module allows Node.js to talk to the operating system and read system details like memory, CPU, user info, platform, etc.
We mainly use the OS module to:
Check system memory
Get CPU information
Know the OS type and platform
Get user details
Monitor system performance



os.platform() → Tells which OS is running (Windows, Linux, mac)
os.arch() → Tells system architecture (32-bit or 64-bit)
os.hostname() → Gives the system/computer name
os.type() → Gives the operating system name
os.release() → Shows OS version
os.totalmem() → Returns total system memory
os.freemem() → Returns available free memory
os.cpus() → Gives CPU core details
os.userInfo() → Used to give user information like username, user ID, group ID, and home directory
os.uptime() → Shows how long the system is running
✅ Example (easy to understand)
const os = require('os');
console.log(os.platform());   // win32
